# Git
.git/
.gitignore
# Nested repositories. A deployment's project is its own checkout under
# projects/, which the Dockerfile copies wholesale — and the pattern above is
# anchored at the context root, so it does not reach projects/<name>/.git.
# Without this the project's entire object store is baked into every image.
**/.git/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
.venv/
venv/
env/
*.egg-info/

# Environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Logs
*.log
logs/

# Deployment script (host tooling, not needed in containers)
deploy.sh

# Scripts (host tooling, not needed in containers)
scripts/

# OS / IDE
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude
CLAUDE.md

# Frontend
# Excluded from the Python image's build context — the web image never
# contains frontend source. At runtime:
#   - dev: ``.:/code`` bind-mount provides the frontend from the host.
#   - prod: docker-compose.prod.yml bind-mounts the host's pre-built
#           frontend/dist and frontend/viewer-dist directories only.
# The on-demand ``frontend-build`` profile service (Node container)
# produces those dist directories on the deploy host; it doesn't need the
# frontend tree to be in the Python image either, since it bind-mounts
# ./frontend directly via its own volume mapping.
#
# Excluding the whole tree here keeps viewer/ (~2 GB with workspace
# node_modules) and any developer-machine residue under node_external/ out
# of the Python image.
frontend/

# SQLite (use proper DB in production)
*.sqlite3

# Django collected statics (managed separately)
static/
staticfiles/
mediafiles/

# Media-file storage dirs (runtime data, not code). A bare `media/`
# pattern here would also exclude the `media` Django app and break the
# production image, which only dev's .:/code bind mount would mask.
media_uploads/
media_staging/

# Local test data (real recordings for the smoke tests). Host-only: the test
# service reaches it through the ``.:/code`` bind mount, so it never needs to be
# in the build context — and must not be, since baking recordings into an image
# would ship patient data.
testdata/

# Format-converter checkouts. A converter for a proprietary format is a separate
# repository the operator clones in and the platform does not track, and it is
# kept out of the image for the same reason it is kept out of the code: one of
# the two known ones is GPLv3, so baking it in would make every image build a
# distribution of it, with the obligations that carries — for a component the
# platform never imports and most deployments do not use. Excluding it also
# keeps ~12 MB out of the image.
#
# A deployment that wants a converter available inside its containers opts in
# deliberately, which is the point. See recordings/README.md → External
# converters for the two ways, and note that neither is `docker exec`: the
# image has no git, runs as a non-root user, and anything installed into a
# running container is lost the next time it is recreated.
#
# Written as exclude-then-re-include rather than as the `*/` form the .gitignore
# rule uses, because Docker does not share git's "directories only" meaning for a
# trailing slash: it cleans the pattern, and `converters/*/` becomes
# `converters/*`, which drops the platform's own converter modules and leaves an
# image that cannot import them.
recordings/converters/*
!recordings/converters/*.py


# Deployment-specific documents (filled-in privacy notices, DPO correspondence,
# operator runbook notes). Host-only: nothing in the image reads them, and they
# name a real organisation and its open compliance gaps.
local/
